1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,057
It's 8 a.m.

2
00:00:01,907 --> 00:00:04,299
The monthly cost review is at 10,

3
00:00:04,149 --> 00:00:07,969
and paid inpatient claims for November just jumped 40%

4
00:00:07,819 --> 00:00:08,649
overnight.

5
00:00:08,499 --> 00:00:10,877
Some claims run-out is normal.

6
00:00:10,727 --> 00:00:13,731
So is this jump real, or is the dashboard wrong?

7
00:00:14,675 --> 00:00:18,249
In healthcare finance, that question lands on an analyst's

8
00:00:18,099 --> 00:00:21,437
desk every month, and a wrong answer moves budgets and

9
00:00:21,287 --> 00:00:21,968
reserves.

10
00:00:21,818 --> 00:00:25,872
DataTrace is a Meta agent that answers it with evidence,

11
00:00:25,722 --> 00:00:26,709
not guesses.

12
00:00:27,643 --> 00:00:29,984
It starts from what the actuary sent:

13
00:00:29,834 --> 00:00:32,354
a voice note and a dashboard screenshot.

14
00:00:32,204 --> 00:00:35,589
Muse Voice Transcribe turns the note into text,

15
00:00:35,439 --> 00:00:38,823
and Muse Spark reads both and drafts the metric

16
00:00:38,673 --> 00:00:42,127
definition: paid claims, November service month,

17
00:00:41,977 --> 00:00:43,297
net of reversals.

18
00:00:44,277 --> 00:00:47,979
Now Muse Spark investigates real-format CMS Medicare

19
00:00:47,829 --> 00:00:50,439
claims, and it never sees a raw row.

20
00:00:50,289 --> 00:00:53,834
Each step is a hypothesis, tested with bounded,

21
00:00:53,684 --> 00:00:56,869
read-only SQL: duplicate eligibility rows,

22
00:00:56,719 --> 00:00:59,975
members dropped by a join, date boundaries,

23
00:00:59,825 --> 00:01:01,854
pending claims, reversals.

24
00:01:01,704 --> 00:01:05,436
It proposes a corrected query, an independent verifier

25
00:01:05,286 --> 00:01:08,753
checks it claim by claim, and a second Meta model,

26
00:01:08,603 --> 00:01:11,075
Muse Wonder, reviews the diagnosis.

27
00:01:11,987 --> 00:01:13,255
Here's the verdict.

28
00:01:13,105 --> 00:01:18,765
Of a $5.21 million jump, $4.56 million is genuine claims

29
00:01:18,615 --> 00:01:19,552
run-out.

30
00:01:19,402 --> 00:01:25,684
$649,400 is a reporting error, across 69 claims.

31
00:01:25,534 --> 00:01:29,397
The eligibility rebuild gave members who switched coverage

32
00:01:29,247 --> 00:01:31,894
two rows, double counting their claims,

33
00:01:31,744 --> 00:01:33,750
and dropped deceased members.

34
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:35,748
The corrected query is verified,

35
00:01:35,598 --> 00:01:39,057
and Muse Wonder writes the brief for the cost review.

36
00:01:40,005 --> 00:01:41,973
When the definition is missing,

37
00:01:41,823 --> 00:01:42,970
it doesn't guess.

38
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:46,041
This request never said how reversals count,

39
00:01:45,891 --> 00:01:48,065
so the agent pauses and asks.

40
00:01:47,915 --> 00:01:50,341
The analyst answers: net paid.

41
00:01:50,191 --> 00:01:53,300
The same investigation resumes where it stopped,

42
00:01:53,150 --> 00:01:55,765
verifies its fix, and is reviewed again.

43
00:01:56,675 --> 00:02:00,950
We ran 360 paid runs against the same Meta model given

44
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:03,013
every raw row and no tools.

45
00:02:02,863 --> 00:02:06,546
On CMS claims, the baseline usually named the right

46
00:02:06,396 --> 00:02:10,426
problem, but put the right dollars on it in only 4 of 16

47
00:02:10,276 --> 00:02:10,773
runs.

48
00:02:10,623 --> 00:02:13,225
The agent: 16 of 16.

49
00:02:13,075 --> 00:02:17,317
At 42,000 rows, the raw data doesn't even fit in the

50
00:02:17,167 --> 00:02:18,497
context window.

51
00:02:19,398 --> 00:02:23,435
We also stress-tested the reviewer by planting errors in

52
00:02:23,285 --> 00:02:25,935
correct diagnoses: inflated amounts,

53
00:02:25,785 --> 00:02:28,295
invented causes, unverified fixes.

54
00:02:28,145 --> 00:02:30,630
Muse Wonder caught 28 of 28.

55
00:02:31,537 --> 00:02:35,261
Four Meta model roles, around a sandboxed SQL engine and a

56
00:02:35,111 --> 00:02:37,109
verifier the model can't fool.

57
00:02:36,959 --> 00:02:39,566
The same engine also runs retail revenue,

58
00:02:39,416 --> 00:02:42,262
and an adapter runs it on open-weight models.

59
00:02:43,199 --> 00:02:44,396
DataTrace.

60
00:02:44,246 --> 00:02:46,931
Evidence before explanations.

61
00:02:46,781 --> 00:02:50,162
Built during the hackathon, and open source on GitHub.
